// Runtime memory allocator: size-class segregated free lists over OS pages.
//
// Every size class, the free-list shape, the carve order and the byte counters
// are LOAD-BEARING, not incidental: the collector is layered directly on this
// heap (runtime/gc/ calls `alloc`/`free`/`liveBytes`), so a "tidier" layout the
// GC does not expect is silent heap corruption, not a style choice.
//
// Free contract: `free` receives the same `size` and
// `alignment` that `alloc` was called with. That is how the GC calls it (object
// sizes live in GC headers), so there are no per-object headers here and no
// address->span lookup table. There IS per-SPAN metadata (#2374), and it needs
// no table either: a span is aligned to its own size, so the span owning a slot
// is `addr & ~(allocSpanBytes - 1)`.
//
// NOT PART OF THE ABI. `runtime/ABI.md` names no allocator symbol: the heap is
// an internal structure reached through `runtime/gc/` and `runtime/root/`'s `g_heap`,
// and the only allocation symbol codegen emits is `bit_rt_gc_alloc`, which is
// the collector's entry point (§6), not this one. Nothing here changes ABI.md.
//
// It is nonetheless `@symbol`-PINNED, and the distinction is worth stating
// because it is easy to get backwards. A pin is not an ABI declaration — it is
// the link-level name, and ABI.md §9's standing rule is about who CALLS a
// function, not about who publishes it. `runtime/gc` imports from here, and each
// module is emitted as its own freestanding archive member (SPEC §17.6), so an
// unpinned name would be spelled `m<id>$f` in gc's object against a bare
// definition in this one and never link. `bit_rt_*` follows `spinlock.bit`'s
// precedent for the same category — a runtime symbol that is not an ABI entry
// point.
//
// THE PINNED SET IS NOT THE IMPORT LIST, and that surprised the first attempt.
// `allocNonZero` is not imported by gc at all, yet needs a pin: at `-O1` the
// inliner splices an imported body into a gc function, and the spliced code
// carries the CALLEE'S OWN calls with it. So the requirement is every function
// gc's *object* ends up referencing, which inlining widens beyond anything
// visible in an import statement. Do not prune this list by reading gc's
// imports — §17.6 refuses the emit and names the missing symbol, so add pins
// until it emits clean rather than predicting the set.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PLATFORM SEAM — why page mapping is NOT done here
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/alloc/` calls `mapPages`/`unmapPages` (mmap/munmap) inline. This module
// CANNOT, and the constraint is spec-level rather than a matter of taste:
//
//   - `syscall` (§11.8) is Linux-only; compiling one for a Darwin target is a
//     hard error at object-emission time.
//   - `extern function` (§11.7) admits, on Linux, only a symbol already defined
//     in the linked `libbitrt.a` (#1444); a libc symbol like the ones here is
//     absent from it and is still E0078 at check time.
//   - Both fire on the DECLARATION, even when the code is never called
//     (verified both ways), so no `hostTarget()` branch can carry both paths.
//   - SPEC §11.7 states the rule outright: "Bit has no arch-conditional
//     compilation, so a program that needs both paths uses `extern function`
//     for Darwin and a raw syscall for Linux, not one source form."
//
// A module is a DIRECTORY (§17.1), so the split is by directory — the same
// shape `runtime/rand` uses for its entropy source (#1368):
//
//   runtime/alloc/          this file: the portable allocator, no platform code
//   runtime/alloc/linux/    mapPages/unmapPages via raw mmap/munmap syscalls
//   runtime/alloc/darwin/   mapPages/unmapPages via libSystem mmap/munmap
//
// The core does not import either one. The platform modules are independent
// leaves, and the CONSUMER binds the pair it wants — so this file compiles
// unchanged for every target, and a per-target `libbitrt.a` archives the core
// plus exactly one page provider.
//
// PAGES ARE THEREFORE SUPPLIED BY THE CALLER, not fetched inline: `refill`
// takes a span address rather than calling mmap itself. All the allocator's
// policy — size classes, classification, alignment, the free-list shape, span
// carving, the byte counters — lives here; only the page fetch crosses out.
//
// LOCK DISCIPLINE. Consequently every mutating function here is a `...Locked`
// form: THE CALLER MUST HOLD THE HEAP LOCK. That is what keeps the port
// honest — the lock must span the refill mmap, and the composed entry point
// holds it across the whole of `alloc`, so a page map still happens inside the
// critical section.
// Splitting the lock instead would let two threads both find a class empty and
// both map a span: benign for correctness, but it would silently double
// `mappedBytes` under contention, and this port does not deviate on an
// observable counter.
//
// THE LOCK ITSELF IS THE CALLER'S. The lock word is passed in as a `*i32`, the
// shape `runtime/spinlock.bit` established. `heapInit` deliberately does NOT
// touch it: a lock is owned by whoever can be holding it, and zeroing one from
// the allocator would silently release a lock another worker holds. The caller
// initializes its own lock word (`atomicStore(lk, spinUnlocked)`), which also
// leaves this module with no imports at all.
//
// Everything is `@nosplit` (§10.3): the allocator must not allocate (it would
// recurse into itself) and must not reach a safepoint (a collection triggered
// while a worker holds the heap lock would deadlock against the very lock the
// collector needs to sweep).
//
// ASSERTIONS ARE ABSENT, and that is forced rather than chosen. Five things the
// caller must honour cannot be checked here:
//
//   alloc:      alignment != 0 and is a power of two
//   allocLarge: alignment <= page size
//   refill:     spanAddr % spanBytes == 0  (#2374 — see `allocSpanBytes`)
//   comptime:   spanBytes >= maxSmall  (every span holds >= 1 slot of every class)
//   comptime:   classSizes[0] >= sizeof(Slot)  (a free slot holds the next-pointer)
//
// A `@nosplit` body cannot panic — `panic` is an out-of-line runtime call that
// can allocate and reach a safepoint — so a failing check has nowhere to go,
// and Bit has no compile-time assertion for the two comptime ones. They are
// instead pinned as runtime properties by `_tests_/stress/alloc`: the power-of-two
// class invariant (b) is what makes any supported alignment satisfiable, the
// carve property (f) proves a span yields `allocSpanSlots(idx)` >= 1 whole
// slots, property (j) checks the span alignment of every span it is handed, and
// class 0 being 8 bytes — exactly one machine word — is checked by the same table
// properties. The GC is the only caller and always passes a valid alignment, so
// nothing is lost beyond the debug aid.
//
// WHAT IS CHECKED, THOUGH, IS THE BOOKKEEPING (#2374). `heapCheckSpansLocked`
// re-derives every class's occupancy from the spans and compares it against the
// counts `take`/`give` maintain, COUNTING violations rather than asserting them —
// the same shape `runtime/gc/gc.bit`'s constraint 5 uses, for the same reason,
// and it stays visible in a release build.

// --- Layout -----------------------------------------------------------------

// Small-object size classes. Every power of two up to the largest class is
// present, which guarantees any supported alignment (a power of two no larger
// than `allocMaxSmall`) is itself a class, so a satisfying slot always exists.
// Requests above the last class are direct-mapped by the caller.
export const allocNumClasses: int = 37
export const allocMaxSmall: int = 16384

// Bytes pulled from the OS each time a size class runs dry. Kept >=
// `allocMaxSmall` so every span holds at least one slot of every class.
//
// EVERY SPAN MUST START ON A MULTIPLE OF THIS, and that is a contract on the
// CALLER of `heapRefillLocked`, not a property this module can enforce: it is
// what makes `allocSpanOf` a mask instead of a lookup. `runtime/gc/mem.bit`'s
// `reserveTake` aligns any take of at least this size to it (#2373), and the
// two test drivers that play the role of the OS do the same.
export const allocSpanBytes: int = 131072

// The heap's state as a count of 8-byte words, so a caller can size the block:
// two counters, one span-list head per class, then the #3045 span cache's
// head and count.
//
// 2 + 37 + 2 IS EXACTLY 41, AND THE BLOCK IS FULL. That is why the class-wide
// bookkeeping #2374 needs — the free-slot list head, the live-slot count and
// the violation count — lives in the anchor SPAN's header rather than here:
// `runtime/root/root.bit` spells the block `[41]i64` and refuses to boot unless
// `heapWords == 41`, so a forty-second word is an edit in another module,
// exactly as a fourth reserve word would be (see `runtime/gc/mem.bit`).
export const heapWords: int = 41

// Exported (#1700): stwPoll inlines heapLiveBytes's one-load body on its hot
// path to avoid a cross-module call, and needs the offset to do it.
export const heapLiveWord: int = 0
const heapMappedWord: int = 1
// One word per class, holding the head of that class's SPAN list — not, since
// #2374, the head of its free-slot list. See `--- Span metadata ---`.
const heapListsWord: int = 2

// #3045: the bounded free-span list's head (0 = empty) and length, reused
// instead of unmapping a reclaimed span and burning reserve address space.
const heapSpanCacheWord: int = 39
const heapSpanCacheCountWord: int = 40

// Max spans the free-span list may hold: 64 * 128 KiB = 8 MiB. THE BOUND IS
// WHAT STOPS THIS RE-INTRODUCING #2325's RSS RATCHET — an uncapped cache of
// reclaimed-but-resident spans is a leak wearing this fix's clothes, just as
// unconditional retention was before #2375 taught the heap to release spans
// at all. 64 is small next to the address-space cost it replaces (#2803: up
// to tens of GB of abandoned reserve chunks) and small next to a live LSP's
// working set, so a cache miss stays rare without the cap itself becoming a
// standing cost.
export const allocSpanCache: int = 64

// The size-class table itself (`allocClassSize`) and the pure helpers built on
// it (`allocAlignUp`, `allocNonZero`, `allocClassify`) live in the sibling
// `./classify.bit` — same module, no import needed. See its header for why.

// --- Pure helpers -----------------------------------------------------------

// The `*i64` addressing the 8-aligned machine word at `addr`.
//
// `*i64` arithmetic scales by 8, so stepping that many elements off the null
// pointer lands exactly on `addr` (§11.4). Every address this module fabricates
// is a span base or a class-sized offset from one, and every class size is a
// multiple of 8, so the division is always exact.
@nosplit fn allocWordAt(addr: int): *i64 {
  let z: *i64 = nil
  return z + (addr / 8)
}

// --- Span metadata ----------------------------------------------------------
//
// `bit lsp --stdio` reached 12.5 GB RSS while idle (#2325) and nothing could
// have given a byte of it back, because a span, once carved, was not
// represented anywhere: its slots went onto one flat per-class list and the
// span itself was forgotten. "Is span S entirely free?" needed an O(n) walk of
// the whole class, and even the answer was useless — the slots are interior
// nodes of a singly-linked list with no back pointers.
//
// A span now carries a header in its own first words, so the question is O(1).
// #2374 made it ANSWERABLE; #2375 answers it and gives the span back.
//
// THE HEADER IS AT THE SPAN'S BASE, AND THE FIRST FEW SLOTS PAY FOR IT. The
// carve starts at the first whole slot past the header (`spanSkip` of them),
// which keeps every slot at an exact multiple of the class size from the span
// base — the alignment argument `allocClassify` rests on, unchanged. Rounding
// the carve start to a fixed byte offset instead would break it for the classes
// whose size is not a power of two.
//
// FIVE OF THE TEN WORDS DESCRIBE THE SPAN. The other five are CLASS-WIDE and
// are meaningful only in the span at the head of the class's list — the
// "anchor" — because `heapWords` is full and has no room for them (see
// `heapWords`). `heapRefillLocked` copies them onto the new anchor as it pushes
// it, which is five stores on a path that already walks a whole span.
//
// `spanOwned` IS A SAFETY WORD, NOT AN OPTIMISATION (#2375). A span is carved
// out of whatever the caller bound as the span reserve, and that is not always
// OS pages this runtime mapped: `boot` binds an 8 MiB arena of its own, and five
// stress programs bind a Bit-MANAGED `[]i64`. `munmap` on a slice the collector
// owns is not a leak, it is a hole in the live heap. So only a span the reserve
// itself got from the OS is ever released, recorded at carve time by the one
// caller that knows.
//
// `spanSpans`/`spanEmpty` EXIST TO KEEP RECLAMATION LINEAR. Reclaiming a span
// means walking the class's flat free list to unlink the slots inside it
// (`heapReclaimSpansLocked`), which is O(free slots) — so firing it once per span
// that empties is quadratic in the span count. Both counters are maintained in
// O(1) by `take`/`give`/`refill`/`reclaim`, and let `heapGiveSmallLocked` ask for
// a pass only when at least HALF the class's spans are releasable: each pass then
// halves the class, so the total walking is linear in the slots freed. Both are
// re-derived and checked by `heapCheckSpansLocked`.
const spanClass: int = 0     // owning size-class index, -1 once doomed
const spanTotal: int = 1     // slots carved out of this span
const spanFree: int = 2      // how many of those are on the class's free list
const spanNext: int = 3      // next span of this class, 0 = end of list
const spanOwned: int = 4     // 1 = the reserve mapped this span, so it may be unmapped
const spanList: int = 5      // ANCHOR ONLY: head of the class's free-slot list
const spanLive: int = 6      // ANCHOR ONLY: slots of the class handed out
const spanBad: int = 7       // ANCHOR ONLY: violations `heapCheckSpansLocked` saw
const spanSpans: int = 8     // ANCHOR ONLY: spans on this class's list
const spanEmpty: int = 9     // ANCHOR ONLY: those that are entirely free AND owned
export const allocSpanHdrWords: int = 10

// Entirely-free owned spans a class must have accumulated before
// `heapGiveSmallLocked` asks for a reclaim pass. THIS IS THE HYSTERESIS, and 2
// is the smallest value that is one: at 1 a class holding a single span would
// ask on every free of its last slot, and the pass would refuse every time
// because the anchor is never released — a walk per free that reclaims nothing.
// At 2 the class has one releasable span beyond the anchor, so every pass that
// runs gives at least one span back.
export const allocSpanKeep: int = 2

// The span owning `addr`. A MASK, NOT A LOOKUP, and that is what keeps
// `heapTakeSmallLocked`/`heapGiveSmallLocked` `@nosplit`-safe and O(1): every
// span starts on a multiple of `allocSpanBytes`, which is a power of two, so
// clearing the low bits of any address inside it lands exactly on its base.
//
// It is sound only because #2373 aligned span-sized takes to `allocSpanBytes`
// rather than to the page. Page alignment would NOT have been enough: on
// aarch64-macos `runtime/root/darwin/boot.bit` passes 4096 as the nominal page
// size while the real page is 16 KiB, so "page-aligned" is ambiguous there, and
// neither reading implies this one.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_alloc_span_of") fn allocSpanOf(addr: int): int {
  return addr & ~(allocSpanBytes - 1)
}

// Slots of size `cs` the header AND the #3176 occupied-slot bitmap
// (`allocBitmapWords`, `./bitmap.bit`) consume at the span base. Rounded UP to
// a whole slot so the carve stays on the class's own grid. The bitmap sits
// immediately after the 10 fixed header words, so this is the only change to
// the span's physical layout the bitmap requires.
@nosplit fn spanSkip(cs: int): int {
  return (allocSpanHdrWords * 8 + allocBitmapWords(cs) * 8 + cs - 1) / cs
}

// Slots a span yields for class `idx`. At least 1 for every class: the largest
// class is `allocMaxSmall` and `allocSpanBytes` is eight times it, so the
// header costs one of those eight slots and seven remain.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_alloc_span_slots") fn allocSpanSlots(idx: int): int {
  let cs = allocClassSize(idx)
  return allocSpanBytes / cs - spanSkip(cs)
}

// `allocSlotIndex` (the #3176/#3177 occupied-slot bitmap's addressing
// primitive) and `allocBitmapWords`/`allocSpanBitmapWord` (the bitmap's
// sizing and a test-only reader) are `./bitmap.bit`, a sibling file — same
// module, no import needed. Split out to keep this file under
// `_tests_/bit/lintfilelines.bit`'s 800-line gate (E0200); it was already at
// exactly that limit before #3176 (see `./spancache.bit`'s header).

// The three per-span fields, read by address. Exposed so a test can compute the
// occupancy tally independently and compare, which is the only way to catch a
// take or give that updated the WRONG span — an error that leaves every
// aggregate over the class exactly right. `allocSpanClass` is pinned
// (`@symbol`, required for a cross-module call under `--freestanding`,
// ABI.md §9): #3177's `gcOwns` reads it to classify a span it has already
// confirmed is presence-keyed, before indexing into its #3176 bitmap.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_alloc_span_class") fn allocSpanClass(spanAddr: int): int {
  let sp = allocWordAt(spanAddr)
  return *(sp + spanClass)
}

export @nosplit fn allocSpanTotal(spanAddr: int): int {
  let sp = allocWordAt(spanAddr)
  return *(sp + spanTotal)
}

export @nosplit fn allocSpanFree(spanAddr: int): int {
  let sp = allocWordAt(spanAddr)
  return *(sp + spanFree)
}

// The next span of the same class, 0 at the end. On the chain
// `heapReclaimSpansLocked` returns this is the next span to release, and the
// caller MUST read it BEFORE unmapping this one — the link lives in the span,
// exactly as a chunk's does in `reserveRelease`.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_alloc_span_next") fn allocSpanNext(spanAddr: int): int {
  let sp = allocWordAt(spanAddr)
  return *(sp + spanNext)
}

// Non-zero when the reserve mapped this span from the OS, so it may be handed
// back. See `spanOwned`.
export @nosplit fn allocSpanOwned(spanAddr: int): int {
  let sp = allocWordAt(spanAddr)
  return *(sp + spanOwned)
}

// Spans on class `idx`'s list, and how many of those are entirely free AND
// releasable — the two counters the reclaim trigger reads. Exported so a test
// can compare them against its own tally over the spans it handed the class,
// the same independence argument the per-span fields are exported for.
export @nosplit fn heapClassSpans(h: *i64, idx: int): int {
  let anchor = *(h + heapListsWord + idx)
  if (anchor == 0) { return 0 }
  let av = allocWordAt(anchor)
  return *(av + spanSpans)
}

export @nosplit fn heapClassEmpty(h: *i64, idx: int): int {
  let anchor = *(h + heapListsWord + idx)
  if (anchor == 0) { return 0 }
  let av = allocWordAt(anchor)
  return *(av + spanEmpty)
}

// --- Heap state -------------------------------------------------------------

// Zero the heap block: null span lists and zeroed counters. Every span the heap
// owned is FORGOTTEN, not freed — the memory is the caller's, so a caller
// reinitialising a heap that has spans must have released them itself.
//
// There are no field defaults to lean on here. The
// caller's block need not have started zeroed, so the stores are explicit.
//
// The lock word is NOT touched — see the header. It belongs to the caller, and
// clearing a lock the allocator does not own would release it out from under a
// holder.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_heap_init") fn heapInit(h: *i64) {
  let i = 0
  while (i < heapWords) {                // bounded: heapWords iterations
    let w = h + i
    *w = 0
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// Bytes handed to callers and not yet freed. Returns to 0 when every allocation
// is freed — the leak metric the stress test checks.
//
// Read atomically and OFF the lock: this is the collector's
// growth trigger (`runtime/gc/` compares it against `next_gc_bytes` outside the
// heap's critical section), so the read must never tear. Bit's atomics are
// sequentially consistent where monotonic would do — strictly
// stronger, never weaker, exactly as `runtime/spinlock.bit` already deviates.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_heap_live_bytes") fn heapLiveBytes(h: *i64): int {
  let p = h + heapLiveWord
  return atomicLoad(p)
}

// Total bytes currently mapped from the OS (spans + large allocations).
export @nosplit fn heapMappedBytes(h: *i64): int {
  let p = h + heapMappedWord
  return atomicLoad(p)
}

// --- Small path -------------------------------------------------------------

// Pop one slot off class `idx`, or 0 when the class is empty and the caller
// must map a span and call `heapRefillLocked`.
//
// CALLER MUST HOLD THE LOCK. A small-class refill happens inline; here the
// empty case returns to the caller because only the caller can reach the OS.
// `0` is safe as the empty sentinel: address 0 is the null page and can never
// be a live slot.
// THE FREE LIST IS STILL ONE FLAT LIST PER CLASS and the pop is still LIFO, so
// the ORDER addresses are handed out in is unchanged (see `heapRefillLocked`).
// Only its head moved: it lives in the anchor span's header, because the heap
// block has no fortieth word to keep it in.
//
// Two invariants make this body's shortcuts safe, and both are established by
// `heapRefillLocked` and preserved by every path here:
//   - a non-zero class word points at a span whose header is initialised, and
//   - the free list's slots all belong to spans on that class's list, so
//     `allocSpanOf` of any of them lands on a real span header.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_heap_take_small_locked") fn heapTakeSmallLocked(h: *i64, idx: int, size: int): int {
  let head = h + heapListsWord + idx
  let anchor = *head
  if (anchor == 0) { return 0 }          // no span yet: the caller must refill
  let a = allocWordAt(anchor)
  let addr = *(a + spanList)
  if (addr == 0) { return 0 }            // every slot handed out: refill
  let slot = allocWordAt(addr)
  *(a + spanList) = *slot                // pop: list = slot.next
  *(a + spanLive) = *(a + spanLive) + 1
  let sp = allocWordAt(allocSpanOf(addr))
  let f = *(sp + spanFree)
  *(sp + spanFree) = f - 1
  // This slot may be the first taken out of an entirely-free span, in which case
  // the span stops being releasable and the class's count of releasable spans
  // must follow it down. Owned-only, so the count stays exactly the number of
  // spans a reclaim pass could act on (#2375).
  if (f == *(sp + spanTotal) && *(sp + spanOwned) != 0) {
    *(a + spanEmpty) = *(a + spanEmpty) - 1
  }
  let live = h + heapLiveWord
  let _ = atomicAdd(live, size)
  return addr
}

// Push a slot back onto class `idx`. CALLER MUST HOLD THE LOCK.
//
// The intrusive next-pointer is written into the freed slot itself, which is
// why `allocClassSize(0)` (8) must be >= one machine word — an assert would catch
// exactly this at comptime (`class_sizes[0] >= @sizeOf(Slot)`).
//
// `addr` MUST have come from a `heapTakeSmallLocked` on the same class, which is
// the allocator's existing free contract stated in the module header. It is what
// makes the class word non-zero here (a slot exists, so its span does) and what
// makes `allocSpanOf(addr)` a span of THIS class.
//
// RETURNS 1 WHEN THE CLASS WANTS A RECLAIM PASS (#2375), 0 otherwise — the
// caller's cue to call `heapReclaimSpansLocked` and unmap what it hands back.
// The decision is POLICY and so lives here with the rest of it (module header),
// is O(1), and is "yes" only when all four hold:
//
//   - this free brought a span to entirely free, and
//   - that span is releasable at all (`spanOwned`), and
//   - it is not the class's anchor. THE ANCHOR IS NEVER RELEASED: it carries the
//     five class-wide words, and a class whose span list emptied to 0 would make
//     the `*head` dereference above read address 0 on the next free. Retaining it
//     is also the "keep one empty span cached" half of the hysteresis — the next
//     allocation of this class needs no map at all.
//   - the class has at least `allocSpanKeep` releasable spans AND they are at
//     least half of its spans, which is what keeps the walking linear
//     (`spanSpans`).
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_heap_give_small_locked") fn heapGiveSmallLocked(h: *i64, addr: int, idx: int, size: int): int {
  let head = h + heapListsWord + idx
  let anchor = *head
  let a = allocWordAt(anchor)
  let slot = allocWordAt(addr)
  *slot = *(a + spanList)                // push: slot.next = list
  *(a + spanList) = addr
  *(a + spanLive) = *(a + spanLive) - 1
  let span = allocSpanOf(addr)
  let sp = allocWordAt(span)
  let f = *(sp + spanFree) + 1
  *(sp + spanFree) = f
  let live = h + heapLiveWord
  let _ = atomicSub(live, size)
  if (f != *(sp + spanTotal) || *(sp + spanOwned) == 0) { return 0 }
  let e = *(a + spanEmpty) + 1
  *(a + spanEmpty) = e
  if (span == anchor) { return 0 }
  if (e < allocSpanKeep) { return 0 }
  if (e * 2 < *(a + spanSpans)) { return 0 }
  return 1
}

// Carve one fresh span of `allocSpanBytes` bytes at `spanAddr` into slots and
// push them onto class `idx`. CALLER MUST HOLD THE LOCK.
//
// `spanAddr` MUST be a multiple of `allocSpanBytes` — see that constant. Nothing
// here can check it (`@nosplit` cannot panic), and a misaligned span does not
// fail: it makes `allocSpanOf` name a NEIGHBOURING span, whose free count then
// drifts while this one's stands still.
//
// Slots are pushed in increasing address order, so the head ends on the HIGHEST
// address. That order is observable (it decides which
// address the next `take` hands out), so it is preserved deliberately.
//
// The new span becomes the class's ANCHOR, so the five class-wide words move
// onto it. The free list is carried over rather than restarted: a refill happens
// when the list is empty, but "empty" is the common case, not a guarantee — a
// caller may refill early, and dropping a non-empty list would strand every slot
// on it.
//
// `owned` MUST say whether `spanAddr` is inside memory the caller's span reserve
// mapped from the OS, because it is what licenses a later `munmap` of this span
// (#2375). Only the caller knows: it is the one that took the span. Passing
// `true` for a span carved out of a caller-supplied arena — the 8 MiB `boot`
// binds, or the Bit-managed `[]i64` five stress programs bind — would eventually
// unmap live heap. When in doubt the answer is `false`: it costs this span's
// 128 KiB for the life of the process and nothing else.
//
// THIS DOES NOT ZERO THE #3176 OCCUPIED-SLOT BITMAP, AND IT MUST BEFORE
// ANYTHING READS IT. `./bitmap.bit` has the proof, the affected class table and
// the measurement that kept it out of #3177.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_heap_refill_locked") fn heapRefillLocked(h: *i64, idx: int, spanAddr: int, owned: bool) {
  let cs = allocClassSize(idx)
  let count = allocSpanBytes / cs - spanSkip(cs)   // >= 1, see `allocSpanSlots`
  let head = h + heapListsWord + idx
  let prevAnchor = *head
  let sp = allocWordAt(spanAddr)
  let own = 0
  if (owned) { own = 1 }
  *(sp + spanClass) = idx
  *(sp + spanTotal) = count
  *(sp + spanFree) = count
  *(sp + spanNext) = prevAnchor
  *(sp + spanOwned) = own
  *(sp + spanList) = 0
  *(sp + spanLive) = 0
  *(sp + spanBad) = 0
  *(sp + spanSpans) = 1
  *(sp + spanEmpty) = own                          // a fresh span is entirely free
  if (prevAnchor != 0) {
    let pa = allocWordAt(prevAnchor)
    *(sp + spanList) = *(pa + spanList)
    *(sp + spanLive) = *(pa + spanLive)
    *(sp + spanBad) = *(pa + spanBad)
    *(sp + spanSpans) = *(pa + spanSpans) + 1
    *(sp + spanEmpty) = *(pa + spanEmpty) + own
  }
  let base = spanAddr + spanSkip(cs) * cs
  let i = 0
  while (i < count) {                    // bounded: <= allocSpanBytes / allocClassSize(0)
    let addr = base + i * cs
    let slot = allocWordAt(addr)
    *slot = *(sp + spanList)
    *(sp + spanList) = addr
    i = i + 1
  }
  *head = spanAddr
  let mapped = h + heapMappedWord
  let _ = atomicAdd(mapped, allocSpanBytes)
}

// Take every entirely-free releasable span off class `idx` and return them as a
// chain linked through `allocSpanNext`, 0 when there is nothing to take. CALLER
// MUST HOLD THE LOCK. This is the function #2375 exists for: before it, no small
// span had any route back to the OS and process RSS was a monotonic high-water
// mark of the sum over 37 classes of each class's all-time peak span count — an
// idle `bit lsp --stdio` reached 12,577 MB that way (#2325).
//
// IT DOES NOT UNMAP, AND THAT IS THE PLATFORM SEAM rather than a division of
// labour: this module cannot contain a `syscall` or a libc `extern` at all (see
// the module header). The caller unmaps each span in the chain — reading
// `allocSpanNext` BEFORE the unmap — and accounts the bytes with
// `heapSpansReleasedLocked`.
//
// TWO PASSES, AND THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. The slots are still interior
// nodes of ONE flat singly-linked free list per class (#2374 kept it
// deliberately: per-span lists need a second head word per class to park full
// spans in, and `heapWords` has none, so they would strand free slots in
// non-head spans — the very leak this fixes). With no back pointers, unlinking a
// span's slots means walking that list. So pass 1 splices ALL the doomed spans
// off the SPAN list, marking each with `spanClass = -1`; pass 2 walks the free
// list ONCE and drops every slot whose span carries the mark. One walk per span
// instead is what makes reclamation quadratic.
//
// The anchor is never a candidate; `heapGiveSmallLocked` says why.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_heap_reclaim_spans_locked") fn heapReclaimSpansLocked(h: *i64, idx: int): int {
  let anchor = *(h + heapListsWord + idx)
  if (anchor == 0) { return 0 }
  let av = allocWordAt(anchor)
  // Bound both walks by memory that exists, exactly as `heapCheckSpansLocked`
  // does: the function that rewrites these links must not hang on a cycle in
  // them.
  let maxSpans = heapMappedBytes(h) / allocSpanBytes + 1
  let doomed = 0
  let removed = 0
  let prevSpan = anchor
  let s = *(av + spanNext)
  let seen = 0
  while (s != 0 && seen < maxSpans) {    // bounded: maxSpans iterations
    let sp = allocWordAt(s)
    let nxt = *(sp + spanNext)
    if (*(sp + spanFree) == *(sp + spanTotal) && *(sp + spanOwned) != 0) {
      let pv = allocWordAt(prevSpan)
      *(pv + spanNext) = nxt
      *(sp + spanClass) = -1             // the mark pass 2 reads
      *(sp + spanNext) = doomed
      doomed = s
      removed = removed + 1
    } else {
      prevSpan = s
    }
    s = nxt
    seen = seen + 1
  }
  if (removed == 0) { return 0 }
  *(av + spanSpans) = *(av + spanSpans) - removed
  *(av + spanEmpty) = *(av + spanEmpty) - removed
  let maxSlots = heapMappedBytes(h) / allocClassSize(idx) + 1
  let prevSlot = 0                       // 0 is never a slot: it means "at the head"
  let cur = *(av + spanList)
  let steps = 0
  while (cur != 0 && steps < maxSlots) { // bounded: maxSlots iterations
    let cw = allocWordAt(cur)
    let nxt = *cw
    let ow = allocWordAt(allocSpanOf(cur))
    if (*(ow + spanClass) < 0) {
      if (prevSlot == 0) {
        *(av + spanList) = nxt
      } else {
        let pw = allocWordAt(prevSlot)
        *pw = nxt
      }
    } else {
      prevSlot = cur
    }
    cur = nxt
    steps = steps + 1
  }
  return doomed
}

// THE INVARIANT, CHECKED RATHER THAN TRUSTED: for every size class,
// `sum over its spans of (total - free)` is the number of its slots currently
// handed out. CALLER MUST HOLD THE LOCK.
//
// An off-by-one here hands a live slot out twice, which is heap corruption that
// surfaces somewhere else entirely and much later, so the two sides are
// maintained by different code: the sum comes from per-span counters that
// `take`/`give` reach through `allocSpanOf`, the live count from a class-wide
// counter they reach through the anchor. A span that is not on its class's list,
// one that is on two, a wrong `total`, a free count out of range, a misaligned
// span base and a slot credited to a span of another class all break the
// equality. What it CANNOT see is a take or give that hit the wrong span of the
// SAME class — that keeps the sum intact — and catching those is
// `_tests_/stress/alloc`'s per-span tally, which is why the per-span fields are
// exported.
//
// THE RECLAIM COUNTERS ARE CHECKED THE SAME WAY (#2375). `spanSpans` and
// `spanEmpty` are maintained in O(1) by `take`/`give`/`refill`/`reclaim` and are
// what decides when a span is handed back to the OS, so they are re-derived here
// from the spans themselves. A drift in `spanSpans` mistimes the trigger; a drift
// in `spanEmpty` is worse — one too high asks for passes that reclaim nothing.
//
// Returns the violations found now, and adds them to each class's anchor so
// `heapSpanViolations` reports every one ever seen. Counting rather than
// asserting is `runtime/gc/gc.bit`'s constraint 5, for its reason: a `@nosplit`
// body cannot panic, and a counter stays visible in a release build.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_heap_check_spans_locked") fn heapCheckSpansLocked(h: *i64): int {
  // Bound the walk by memory that exists: a span is `allocSpanBytes` of mapped
  // memory, so no class can own more spans than the heap has mapped in total.
  // The one function whose job is to distrust these links must not hang on a
  // cycle in them.
  let maxSpans = heapMappedBytes(h) / allocSpanBytes + 1
  let found = 0
  let idx = 0
  while (idx < allocNumClasses) {        // bounded: allocNumClasses iterations
    let anchor = *(h + heapListsWord + idx)
    if (anchor != 0) {
      let want = allocSpanSlots(idx)
      let bad = 0
      let inUse = 0
      let seen = 0
      let empty = 0
      let s = anchor
      while (s != 0 && seen < maxSpans) {   // bounded: maxSpans iterations
        let sp = allocWordAt(s)
        if (s != allocSpanOf(s)) { bad = bad + 1 }
        if (*(sp + spanClass) != idx) { bad = bad + 1 }
        if (*(sp + spanTotal) != want) { bad = bad + 1 }
        let f = *(sp + spanFree)
        if (f < 0 || f > want) { bad = bad + 1 }
        if (f == want && *(sp + spanOwned) != 0) { empty = empty + 1 }
        inUse = inUse + want - f
        s = *(sp + spanNext)
        seen = seen + 1
      }
      if (s != 0) { bad = bad + 1 }      // ran out of bound: a cycle or a bogus link
      let av = allocWordAt(anchor)
      if (inUse != *(av + spanLive)) { bad = bad + 1 }
      if (seen != *(av + spanSpans)) { bad = bad + 1 }
      if (empty != *(av + spanEmpty)) { bad = bad + 1 }
      *(av + spanBad) = *(av + spanBad) + bad
      found = found + bad
    }
    idx = idx + 1
  }
  return found
}

// Every violation `heapCheckSpansLocked` has recorded on this heap. Zero on a
// heap it has never run against, so a test must drive the check itself rather
// than read this and conclude the heap was verified.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_heap_span_violations") fn heapSpanViolations(h: *i64): int {
  let total = 0
  let idx = 0
  while (idx < allocNumClasses) {        // bounded: allocNumClasses iterations
    let anchor = *(h + heapListsWord + idx)
    if (anchor != 0) {
      let av = allocWordAt(anchor)
      total = total + *(av + spanBad)
    }
    idx = idx + 1
  }
  return total
}

// Account the release of `nbytes` of SPAN memory the caller has unmapped.
// CALLER MUST HOLD THE LOCK.
//
// The mirror of the `heapRefillLocked` accounting, and the counterpart of
// `heapLargeFreeLocked` for the small path. It exists because spans became
// releasable in bulk (#2373): the collector's span reserve now remembers the
// chunks it mapped, and `gcDeinit` unmaps them, so the mapped count must follow
// them out or it would claim memory the process no longer has.
//
// It does NOT touch `liveBytes` and does not touch the free lists, because by
// the time it is called the slots are already off them. Two callers, and both
// must have done that first: `gcDeinit`, which is releasing whole chunks at
// teardown, and the per-span reclaim path (#2375), whose `heapReclaimSpansLocked`
// unlinked the slots and the span before its caller unmapped a single byte.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_heap_spans_released_locked") fn heapSpansReleasedLocked(h: *i64, nbytes: int) {
  let mapped = h + heapMappedWord
  let _ = atomicSub(mapped, nbytes)
}

// The #3045 span cache (`heapSpanCachePushLocked`/`heapSpanCacheTakeLocked`)
// is `./spancache.bit`, a sibling file — same module (SPEC §17.1), no import
// needed. Split out to keep this file under `_tests_/bit/lintfilelines.bit`'s
// 800-line gate (E0200).

// --- Large path -------------------------------------------------------------

// The mapped length a direct-mapped request of `size` occupies, given page size
// `pageSize`. Split out from the accounting so the caller can map exactly this
// many bytes and free exactly the same number back.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_alloc_large_len") fn allocLargeLen(size: int, pageSize: int): int {
  return allocAlignUp(allocNonZero(size), pageSize)
}

// Account a direct-mapped allocation of `size` bytes occupying `nbytes` mapped
// bytes. CALLER MUST HOLD THE LOCK.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_heap_large_alloc_locked") fn heapLargeAllocLocked(h: *i64, size: int, nbytes: int) {
  let mapped = h + heapMappedWord
  let _ = atomicAdd(mapped, nbytes)
  let live = h + heapLiveWord
  let _ = atomicAdd(live, size)
}

// Account the release of a direct-mapped allocation. CALLER MUST HOLD THE LOCK.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_heap_large_free_locked") fn heapLargeFreeLocked(h: *i64, size: int, nbytes: int) {
  let mapped = h + heapMappedWord
  let _ = atomicSub(mapped, nbytes)
  let live = h + heapLiveWord
  let _ = atomicSub(live, size)
}
